:258,"repeatable":true}},{"kind":"construction","group":"construction","case":"locale_with_numerals","detail":"Locale::builder() with grouping, thousands and numerals","once":{"allocations":17,"reallocations":0,"bytes":481,"repeatable":true}}]},"notes":["Machine-specific and not a regression gate. No CI job reads this file; it is the archived observation MIGRATION_TO_RUST.md section 9 asks for, taken on one developer host with whatever else that host was doing at the time.","The difference between the owned_output and reused_output groups is one allocation and one deallocation per call, and on this host that is close to the run-to-run spread of an individual case. Read the direction across the group rather than any single delta, and expect the effect to clear the spread only where the pair is a large share of the call: the type c text case, which does no numeric conversion at all, and the kilobyte binary radix case. The exact form of the comparison is in the allocation profile below, where the reused path makes exactly one fewer allocation in every case measured.","The allocation numbers are exact GlobalAlloc call counts for one call, not statistics. They support \"this case made N allocations on this build\" and nothing stronger. They do not establish that any path is allocation-free, they say nothing about peak resident memory or fragmentation, and another allocator, optimization level or toolchain may produce different counts for the same code.","Every reused-output measurement ran against a String reserved to the exact output length outside the timed loop, and the benchmark asserts that the capacity was unchanged afterwards, so a reallocation counted on a reused row grew a buffer inside the conversion rather than the output buffer supplied by the caller.","The counting allocator is installed for the whole process. Recording is off during the timed portion, which costs one relaxed atomic load per allocation, so these times are not exactly those of a build without the instrumentation."]}
